From 5e00cec88952b1c97ddb84429b120162e2874dd0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:35:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] guiinfo: the temperatures are read away from the thread that
 presents the video

Opening the player information over a video held the thread that draws
the GUI, which also presents the frames of the video, for about 100 ms
on an RTX 4080 SUPER, at every open: two or three frames that did not
change (kodi.log: "DialogPlayerProcessInfo.xml opened over a video in
97.5-112.0 ms ... 97.3-111.4 ms to open").

The panel shows System.CPUTemperature, in a visible condition that is
evaluated when the dialog opens (SetInitialVisibility()). For any of
the heat labels CSystemGUIInfo read both temperatures, once a minute,
there and then: the GPU's through the gputempcommand of LibreELEC's
advancedsettings.xml, /usr/bin/gputemp, which on an NVIDIA card runs
"nvidia-smi -q -x" and waits for it (popen()/pclose()). Measured in
QEMU with the image of 3ea7eec: with LibreELEC's gputemp (4-7 ms there,
it only reads hwmon) an open costs 5.2-6.1 ms "to open" where a reading
is due and 0.5 ms within the minute after one; with a gputempcommand
that takes 100 ms, 104.9-106.5 ms and 0.5 ms: the pattern of the
RTX 4080 SUPER's log.

Now:
- only the temperature asked for is read: System.CPUTemperature no
  longer runs the GPU's command, nor do the fan speed and the use of
  the processors;
- on the GUI's thread a reading never waits (CBackgroundReading): the
  last one is given at once and, where it is a minute old or there is
  none, a new one runs on the job manager, one at a time; before a
  first one has ended the label is empty, not "Not available";
- any other thread (an add-on, JSON-RPC) waits for a reading that is
  due, as Kodi did, so that a script that reads the label once still
  gets a value;
- kodi.log says how long the first reading of each temperature took
  and on which thread ("System information: the CPU temperature read
  in ... ms").

With this change the same opens cost 0.4-1.6 ms "to open", with either
command, and with a cputempcommand that takes 100 ms the reading takes
its 104 ms on a thread of the job manager while the open costs 0.5-0.8
ms. A label read through JSON-RPC still gets its value.

CBackgroundReading keeps its state in an object shared with the task,
so a reading that ends after Kodi has begun to stop writes into nothing
else. TestBackgroundReading covers: no wait without wait, one reading
at a time, the last reading while the next runs, a waiting caller that
reads there, a runner that cannot run, a task that outlives its owner
and a later reading kept over an earlier one.
